const phoneHref = `tel:+1${phoneRaw}`;
const canonical = "https://samedayappliance.repair/services/refrigerator-repair/whirlpool-not-cooling/";

const title = "Whirlpool Refrigerator Not Cooling? Causes & Fix — Same Day";
const description = "Whirlpool refrigerator not cooling? Usually the evaporator fan, a compressor start relay, the defrost board, or dirty coils. Safe checks and when to call.";

const faqs = [
  {
    q: "Why is my Whirlpool refrigerator not cooling?",
    a: "On a Whirlpool that runs but won't cool, the usual causes are a failed evaporator fan (which shows as 'freezer cold, fridge warm'), a compressor start relay or overload that won't let the compressor start, an adaptive-defrost control board fault that ices the evaporator, dirty condenser coils, and a bad thermistor. Cleaning the coils and checking airflow are yours to do; the start relay, board, and sealed system are a tech job. Whirlpool builds Maytag, KitchenAid, and Amana on the same platform, so this diagnosis applies to those too. If it's clearly warm inside, call the same day — your food is on a clock."
  },
  {
    q: "My Whirlpool clicks every few minutes but won't get cold — what's that?",
    a: "That click-click-click every three to five minutes is the classic sign of a failed compressor start relay (or its overload). The relay is supposed to help the compressor start; when it fails, the compressor tries to start, draws too much current, the overload trips, and you hear the click as it keeps retrying. It's a common, relatively inexpensive part — and catching it early can save the compressor from damage. Diagnosing and replacing the relay is a tech job, but it's usually a one-visit fix, and much cheaper than the compressor it protects."
  },
  {
    q: "My Whirlpool freezer is cold but the fridge is warm — what's wrong?",
    a: "That split — cold freezer, warm fridge — is the classic evaporator fan failure. The evaporator fan blows cold air from the freezer coil up into the fridge; when it fails or frost from a defrost-board fault jams it, the freezer stays cold but the fridge warms. On Whirlpool, an adaptive-defrost control board that's stopped running the defrost cycle is a common root cause of the frost that jams the fan. Both are fixable, tech-level repairs inside the cabinet. Keep the doors closed while you wait — the fridge food is on roughly a four-hour clock."
  },
  {
    q: "Can I fix a Whirlpool refrigerator that's not cooling myself?",
    a: "Some of it. Vacuum the condenser coils (behind the rear panel or under a front kick-plate), make sure interior vents aren't blocked, confirm the doors seal and the temperature is set right, pull the fridge a few inches off the wall to shed heat, and give it a power-cycle. What you should not do is open the sealed refrigeration system, test or replace the start relay, or swap the control board — those carry shock and refrigerant risks and are a tech's job. If it stays warm after the coil clean, call the same day."
  },
  {
    q: "How long is food safe in a Whirlpool that stopped cooling?",
    a: "Per USDA and FDA guidance, a refrigerator keeps food safe for about 4 hours with the door kept closed, and a full freezer holds a safe temperature for roughly 48 hours (24 hours if half-full) if you keep it shut. Perishable food — meat, poultry, seafood, eggs, milk, cut produce, leftovers — held above 40°F for 2 hours or more should be thrown out. When in doubt, throw it out, and never taste food to check. That 4-hour fridge window is why a no-cool refrigerator is a same-day call — keep the doors closed and get a tech out before it closes."
  }
];

