<ServiceHero
  section="services"
  slug="refrigerator-repair/whirlpool-not-cooling"
  eyebrow="Whirlpool Refrigerator Not Cooling · Diagnostic Guide · Same Day Southern California"
  title="Whirlpool Refrigerator Not Cooling? Start With the Coils and Fan"
  subtitle="Most Whirlpool no-cool calls are the evaporator fan, a compressor start relay, the defrost board, or dirty coils. Here's the cause list, what you can safely do, and how long your food stays safe."
  heroImageTitle="Whirlpool Refrigerator Not Cooling Repair"
  showBranches={true}
/>

<!-- 01 ANSWER-FIRST -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">01 · The short answer</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">A running-but-warm Whirlpool is usually the fan, the relay, or the coils.</h2>

    <p><strong>When a Whirlpool refrigerator runs but won't cool, the causes are a short list: a failed evaporator fan ("freezer cold, fridge warm"), a compressor start relay or overload that won't let the compressor start (you'll hear it click every few minutes), an adaptive-defrost control board fault that ices the evaporator, dirty condenser coils, or a bad thermistor. Cleaning the coils and checking airflow are safe to do yourself; the relay, board, and sealed system are a tech job. Treat a no-cool fridge as a same-day call — your food is on a clock. $89 residential diagnostic, waived with repair.</strong></p>

    <p class="callout"><strong>Food is on a clock:</strong> per USDA and FDA guidance, a refrigerator keeps food safe for about 4 hours with the door closed. Keep the doors shut, move critical items to a cooler with ice if you can, and get a tech out before that window closes. Full food-safety details are in the section below.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 02 PLATFORM -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">02 · One platform, four badges</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Whirlpool, Maytag, KitchenAid, Amana — same cooling parts.</h2>

    <p>Whirlpool, Maytag, KitchenAid, and Amana refrigerators are all Whirlpool Corporation brands built on the same platform, sharing the cooling architecture: the evaporator fan, the compressor and its start relay and overload, the adaptive-defrost control board, and the thermistor. Many <strong>Kenmore</strong> refrigerators — the ones whose model number starts with <strong>106</strong> — are Whirlpool-built and use the same parts. So a no-cool diagnosis is essentially identical across all of them, which is why we treat them as one family and stock the parts that fit all of them.</p>

    <p>The brand-specific angles differ — Maytag's longer parts warranty, KitchenAid's built-in models, Amana's value line, and the "who actually built my Kenmore" model lookup — and we cover those on the <a href="/services/refrigerator-repair/maytag-not-cooling/">Maytag</a>, <a href="/services/refrigerator-repair/kitchenaid-not-cooling/">KitchenAid</a>, <a href="/services/refrigerator-repair/amana-not-cooling/">Amana</a>, and <a href="/services/refrigerator-repair/kenmore-not-cooling/">Kenmore</a> not-cooling guides. The diagnosis below applies to all of them.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 03 CAUSES -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">03 · Why a Whirlpool stops cooling</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">The causes, in the order our techs check them.</h2>

    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Dirty condenser coils (check first)</h3>
      <p>Coils caked in dust and pet hair can't shed the compressor's heat, so the fridge slowly loses cooling. It's the most common homeowner-fixable cause, and it matters more in an LA summer.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Compressor start relay — the "clicking" fault</h3>
      <p>A failed start relay or overload makes the compressor try to start, trip, and retry, so you hear a click every few minutes and the fridge won't get cold. It's an inexpensive part and catching it early can save the compressor. A tech job, but usually a one-visit fix.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Evaporator fan — "freezer cold, fridge warm"</h3>
      <p>The evaporator fan blows cold air from the freezer coil up into the fridge. When it fails or frost jams it, the freezer stays cold but the fridge warms. A common, fixable fault, and a tech job inside the cabinet.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Adaptive-defrost control board</h3>
      <p>Whirlpool's defrost board runs the periodic defrost cycle. When it fails, frost builds on the evaporator until air can't pass and cooling drops — often the real reason an evaporator fan is jammed with ice.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Thermistor or sealed system</h3>
      <p>A misreading thermistor can stop the fridge from calling for cooling; a failed compressor or sealed-system leak is the serious, higher-ticket end. Both are tech diagnoses.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 04 SAFE FIX -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">04 · What you can safely check</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Try these before the food is at risk.</h2>

    <ol class="diag-list">
      <li><strong>Vacuum the condenser coils</strong> behind the rear panel or under a front kick-plate. This alone fixes a lot of gradual no-cool cases.</li>
      <li><strong>Clear the interior vents</strong> so food isn't blocking airflow between the freezer and fridge.</li>
      <li><strong>Check the door seal and temperature setting</strong> — confirm the doors close fully and the setting wasn't bumped warm.</li>
      <li><strong>Give it room and a power-cycle</strong> — pull the fridge a few inches off the wall, then unplug a minute and restart.</li>
      <li><strong>Listen for a repeating click,</strong> which points at the start relay, and note whether the freezer is cold while the fridge is warm.</li>
    </ol>

    <p class="callout"><strong>Where to stop:</strong> coils, vents, seal, temperature, and a power-cycle are safe. Do not open the sealed refrigeration system, test or replace the start relay, or swap the control board — those carry shock and refrigerant risks and are our techs' work.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 05 FOOD SAFETY -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">05 · Heat-wave food safety — when to call today</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">A warm fridge is a clock. Here are the numbers.</h2>

    <p>A refrigerator that's stopped cooling is a race against the clock, and in an LA heat wave that clock runs faster. Per USDA and FDA guidance:</p>

    <ul class="diag-list">
      <li><strong>Refrigerator: about 4 hours</strong> of safe food with the door kept closed.</li>
      <li><strong>Freezer: roughly 48 hours full, 24 hours half-full,</strong> if the door stays shut.</li>
      <li><strong>Above 40°F for 2 hours or more → discard it.</strong> That covers meat, poultry, seafood, eggs, milk, cut produce, and leftovers.</li>
      <li><strong>When in doubt, throw it out</strong> — and never taste food to decide. Frozen food can be safely refrozen only if it still has ice crystals or is at 40°F or below.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>That's why a no-cool Whirlpool is a same-day call. Keep the doors closed, move the critical items to a cooler with ice, and get a tech out before the four-hour fridge window closes. We answer phones 24/7 and run same-day across most of the area. <strong>$89 residential diagnostic, waived with the repair.</strong></p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 06 TECH + LINKS -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">06 · When to call us</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Coils clean, still warm? Now it's a diagnostic.</h2>

    <p>When the coils are clean, the vents are clear, and the Whirlpool still won't cool, the fault is inside — the start relay, the evaporator fan, the defrost board, a thermistor, or the sealed system. Our techs test each in order and carry the common Whirlpool start relays, fan motors, and defrost boards for a one-visit fix where possible.</p>

    <p>More help: the general <a href="/services/refrigerator-repair/not-cooling/">refrigerator not cooling</a> guide (all brands), and if it's the fridge warm while the freezer's fine, <a href="/services/refrigerator-repair/fridge-not-cooling-freezer-works/">fridge not cooling, freezer works</a>. Same-platform siblings: <a href="/services/refrigerator-repair/maytag-not-cooling/">Maytag</a>, <a href="/services/refrigerator-repair/kitchenaid-not-cooling/">KitchenAid</a>, <a href="/services/refrigerator-repair/amana-not-cooling/">Amana</a>, <a href="/services/refrigerator-repair/kenmore-not-cooling/">Kenmore</a>. Brand coverage: <a href="/brands/whirlpool-refrigerator-repair/">Whirlpool refrigerator repair</a>. Same-day across <a href="/los-angeles/refrigerator-repair/">Los Angeles</a>, <a href="/pasadena/refrigerator-repair/">Pasadena</a>, and <a href="/rancho-cucamonga/refrigerator-repair/">Rancho Cucamonga</a> in most cases. <a href="/book/">Book online</a> or call {phone}.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 07 FAQ -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">07 · FAQ</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Whirlpool refrigerator not cooling: common questions.</h2>

    <div class="faq-list">
